// tailnet-acl.hujson — A REFERENCE TO PASTE INTO THE ADMIN CONSOLE.
//
// NOTHING READS THIS FILE. Tailscale's policy lives server-side in the
// coordination service; the daemon fetches it from there and never looks at
// local disk. This file is not deployed to the hub, is not consumed by
// tailscaled, and is not uploaded by anything. It is a document.
//
// An earlier version of this file WAS deployed to the hub, and the setup
// script "verified" it by reading it. That check was backwards and has been
// removed: it confirmed that a file written here said what was written here,
// which is true regardless of what policy is actually in force. Real
// verification would need a tailnet API key, and that key can rewrite who
// reaches the whole LAN — not worth carrying to catch the Owner's own console
// edits (standing threat model, ruled 2026-08-09).
//
// THE EARLIER VERSION WAS ALSO BROKEN, IN TWO WAYS WORTH RECORDING:
//   1. It put `autogroup:admin` inside a `groups` entry. Group members must be
//      full email addresses; an autogroup cannot be one. That is a hard syntax
//      error and the console rejects it.
//   2. Every rule targeted `tag:homehub:*`, but the hub authenticated AS A USER
//      and carries no tag. With deny-by-omission, that policy accepts nothing —
//      applying it would have cut off tunnel access to the hub entirely.
// Validate anything here in the console's preview before saving it.
//
// DO YOU NEED A POLICY AT ALL? On a single-user tailnet the default is "every
// member reaches everything", and every member is the Owner's own device — so
// the default means your laptop reaches your hub. That is fine. This becomes
// worth applying when a second person, a shared node or a tagged service
// appears, which is the point at which "every node" stops meaning "my nodes".
{
  "acls": [
    // The Owner's own devices reach each other.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src":    ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst":    ["autogroup:self:*"],
    },

    // The LAN reached THROUGH the hub's subnet route. A subnet route is not
    // self-authorising: traffic to a LAN host is evaluated by this policy
    // first, so removing this rule removes LAN access even while the route
    // stays advertised and approved in the console.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src":    ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst":    ["192.168.117.0/24:*"],
    },
  ],

  // Deny by omission: anything not accepted above is refused. There is no
  // trailing allow-all and one should not be added "temporarily".

  // No Tailscale SSH. The hub already has key-only SSH under its own
  // requirement, and a second authentication path to the same box is a second
  // thing to get wrong and a second thing to revoke.
  "ssh": [],
}
